WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.160
Film Courage: Kathie, what are some fatal
errors that a writer should avoid at all costs?

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:12.170
Kathie Fong Yoneda: [Laughs] Wow!

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:20.130
I think one of the things is that you need
to know that it’s not just one script.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:25.289
You have to think about your whole career,
that you don’t just bank all your hopes

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:31.619
on this one piece of material, that you have
to be working constantly and that things don’t

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:33.480
happen just like that.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.380
One of the things I did in my book was I took
a survey.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.850
I asked all of writers who came into the studios
and that I worked with a couple questions.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.170
And I asked them…and one of them was “How
long did it take you?

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:51.570
And how many scripts did you have to write
before something major happened like you got

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.430
an agent, you got an option or somebody bought
something?”

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.190
I interviewed over 200 professional writers.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.780
These are not waiter writers.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.850
These are professional writers.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.259
Only one got her script sold…the first script!

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:12.490
Film Courage: Wow…Kathie Fong Yoneda: And
she said it took her five years to get a second

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.899
sale.

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:20.810
And the average actually turned out to be
eight tor nine [scripts].

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.380
Film Courage: Eight or nine….?

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.380
Kathie Fong Yoneda: Scripts.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.430
So you have to love what you do.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.240
You don’t want to be a one-script-wonder.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.479
It doesn’t happen very often where somebody
only writes one script and then that’s it

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:35.659
and then nothing.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:38.469
They have to be writing constantly.

00:01:38.469 --> 00:01:44.179
And that is what agents, executives and producers
are looking for.

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:45.819
They want to build a relationship.

00:01:45.819 --> 00:01:53.649
You’ll notice that people like Spielberg
and all the big directors.

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:55.579
They all have the same credits.

00:01:55.579 --> 00:02:04.539
All the same people for cinematographer, script
supervisor, and they go to some of the same

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:08.940
writers because they have built a relationship
with them.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.129
Because they like their kind of storytelling.

00:02:12.129 --> 00:02:19.060
Their characters speak the same kind of language
that they would like to convey to movie viewers.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:27.099
And so being able to have those kind of relationships,
it’s not just writing, it’s also writing

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:31.470
the kind of things that are going to continue
that relationship.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:38.560
So I love the fact that most of the writers
nowadays have a better understanding and now

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.959
when I see them at Scriptfest or Great American
Pitchfest…when I ask them…I always ask

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:45.430
them “What else are you working on?”

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:50.670
just to test them nowadays most of them will
say that they have more than one.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.129
So I guess it’s working.

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:56.830
I guess they are getting used to wanting to
have more than one piece of material.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:57.330
Question for the Viewers: How many scripts
have you written?

